- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars have lower Vmag values. Giants can be bright but may appear dim if distant; alone, Vmag is not a strong indicator.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star. Giants tend to be luminous and often farther away, so smaller parallax may hint at giants.
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance; less useful directly for classification.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature/color. Giants tend to have redder colors (higher B-V) especially if they are K or M type giants.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class (V = main sequence, III = giant) is embedded here. Presence of III or III/IV indicates giant.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Absolute magnitude is intrinsic brightness. Giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of the same spectral type; smaller Amag values (more negative or smaller positive) indicate giants.

